VEGETARIAN DIET — excludes all meat, fish and poultry; usually includes dairy/eggs (lacto-ovo). Pros: better overall diet quality and higher intake of fiber, vitamin C, vitamin E and magnesium; weight loss (a review of 12 studies found ~4.5 lb more loss over 18 weeks, and in a 6-month study of 74 people with type 2 diabetes it was nearly twice as effective as a low-calorie diet; lower BMI in ~61,000 adults); possibly lower cancer risk (breast, colon, rectum); better blood-sugar control and ~53% lower diabetes risk in one study; improved cholesterol/LDL. Cons: needs planning to meet B12, iron and omega-3s — it only supplies ALA omega-3 (from walnuts/flaxseed), which the body converts to EPA/DHA poorly.

HEALTHLINE'S BOTTOM LINE: A pescatarian diet is a healthy choice and offers 'more flexibility and some additional nutrition compared to a standard vegetarian diet' — the seafood supplies EPA/DHA omega-3s (good for brain and heart) plus vitamin B12, vitamin D, iodine and lean protein that strict vegetarian/vegan diets can lack, so nutrient needs are easier to meet. A well-planned vegetarian diet is also healthy (linked to weight loss, better blood sugar, cholesterol and lower chronic-disease risk) but requires more care to cover B12/iron/omega-3s. For eating healthier, the pescatarian diet is the easier-to-follow option that adds heart- and brain-friendly omega-3s, provided you choose fish wisely to limit mercury; vegetarians can get similar benefits with planning, while a flexitarian approach is a more flexible middle ground."}

…t. A vegetarian eating pattern that adds fish/seafood; it excludes red meat and poultry but may include dairy and eggs, built on whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds, fruits and vegetables. Health upsides: pescatarian women gained ~2.5 lb (1.1 kg) less per year than meat-eaters; diabetes risk 4.8% vs 7.6% for omnivores; ~22% lower risk of dying from heart disease; supplies omega-3 EPA/DHA (oily fish like salmon, sardines) plus vitamin B12, zinc and selenium (e.g., oysters, mussels, cod = 19 g protein/3 oz with <1 g fat). Watch-outs: mercury in large fish (avoid shark, swordfish, tilefish, king mackerel; limit albacore/yellowfin tuna to <=1 serving/week, light tuna 2-3/week); can be unhealthy if based on processed starches, junk food and fish sticks.

2) MEDITERRANEAN DIET — https://www.healthline.com/nutrition/mediterranean-diet-meal-plan. The leading pescatarian-style fish-based diet: plant-forward with fish/seafood as the main animal protein, plus extra-virgin olive oil, nuts/seeds, whole grains, legumes, fruits and vegetables; allows eggs, dairy, small amounts of poultry/red meat and a little red wine; limits added sugar, refined grains, processed meat and highly processed foods.

VERDICT: Healthline/AHA rank the pescatarian diet in the highest (Tier 1) heart-health tier (score >=85), alongside the Mediterranean and vegetarian diets. The pescatarian diet is the stricter, fully meat-free fish-based option with a clear omega-3 boost; the Mediterranean diet is more flexible and easier to sustain long term. For eating healthier, both are excellent — pick the pescatarian diet to cut out all meat/poultry, or the Mediterranean diet if you prefer flexibility. Healthline's dedicated comparison (Vegetarian vs. Vegan vs. Pescatarian) also shows pescatarian is more flexible than vegetarian/vegan and makes it easier to meet nutrient needs, and Healthline's low-carb 'ketotarian' diet is a pescatarian keto variant. No sign-in, account, payment or personal data was required."}

